laurel 3071: LAUREL SCHED PLACE: without CLPC every thread group prefers the boot pset (gold) for every QoS bucket and the E -> P edge forbids migration, so silver runs only gold's spill and the UI's threads queue behind the daemons on gold (boot storm: 367 s busy of 480, usblog 2026-11-07 05:21). Now, as CLPC on an iPhone: the utility and background buckets prefer the efficiency pset (silver); fixed-priority, foreground, user-initiated and default keep gold; both edges carry the weighted spill (64 us), so an idle cluster takes the other's excess and no job or QoS is limited to a cluster. Set once when every CPU has registered (before launchd), applied to the thread groups that exist and to every later one. Pure rule in osfmk/kern/laurel_sched_place.h (host test scripts/laurel/test-sched-place.sh); the sched_clutch.c hunks are post/9027 (the base owns sched_clutch.c on xnu-12377). Kill switch laurelschedplace=0. docs/laurel_sprout/cpu-efficiency.md.

diff --git a/osfmk/kern/laurel_sched_place.h b/osfmk/kern/laurel_sched_place.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dd47076
--- /dev/null
+++ b/osfmk/kern/laurel_sched_place.h
@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+/*
+ * laurel 3071: LAUREL SCHED PLACE: the pset a QoS bucket prefers, without kernel types, so that the host test
+ * (scripts/laurel/test-sched-place.sh) runs exactly this rule. As CLPC on an iPhone: utility and background work prefers the
+ * efficiency cluster; fixed-priority, foreground, user-initiated and default work prefers the performance cluster (the boot pset).
+ * The edges stay open both ways (sched_clutch.c), so this is a preference, not a limit.
+ */
+#ifndef LAUREL_SCHED_PLACE_H
+#define LAUREL_SCHED_PLACE_H
+static inline unsigned
+lsp_pset_for_bucket(unsigned bucket, unsigned bucket_ut, unsigned bucket_bg, unsigned p_pset, unsigned e_pset)
+{
+	return (bucket == bucket_ut || bucket == bucket_bg) ? e_pset : p_pset;
+}
+#endif /* LAUREL_SCHED_PLACE_H */
